     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "strong",
        "v": "Cost per completed task beats cost per token."
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": " The cost-optimization guide says: \"Price lists are written per token, and per token the frontier model looks expensive... You pay for completed tasks, though, so compare models on cost per completed task.\" A more capable model finishes with fewer turns, less searching, and less backtracking. [5]"
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "strong",
        "v": "Worked calculation."
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": " On SWE-bench Pro, Fable 5.1 at "
       },
       {
        "t": "code",
        "v": "low"
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": " effort solved 88.6% of tasks for $0.54 per solved task. Sonnet 5 at its default solved 77.4% for $0.84 per solved task, despite a per-token price five times lower. Fable 5.1 delivered 11 more points for 35% less per solved task. The sticker price said the opposite. [5]"
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": "Cost per completed task is total spend divided by successes. If 100 tasks cost $50 and 80 succeed, cost per completed task is $0.625, not $0.50. Failures and retries are why the two numbers diverge. [5]"
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "strong",
        "v": "Where it goes wrong."
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": " A team estimates cost from character counts, ignores cache reads, and reports cost per call. A cheap model that fails 30% of the time and needs retries can cost more per outcome than the expensive one. [5]"
       }
      ]
     }
